⚠️ **IMPORTANT (thread `PRRT_kwDORYAuYs61syQ7`) — fixed in <commit>**

Agreed — the failed head probe is none of the four documented invisible-marker causes, and the ladder's rule 1 ("marker present → its verdict governs; no downgrade in any case") must not be defeatable by a transient `gh pr view --json headRefOid` failure. Implemented the fail-safe side of your suggested guard (suppress-approve), since a loud needs-human escalation per transient blip would page a human on every rate limit; the genuine approve still re-arrives on a re-run once gh recovers and the marker resolves for the head.

`probeMachineVerdictMarker` now records the state and names it loudly:

```js
if (!records.length) return null;
var headSha = ctx.pr ? headShaOf(io, opts.repo, ctx.pr) : '';
if (ctx.pr && !headSha) {
    // trusted records exist but WHICH head they sit on is unknown
    ctx.headUnresolvable = true;
    ctx.log('::warning::machine verdict record(s) exist but the PR head sha is unresolvable (probe failure) — the legacy parser may decide on its safe side only (no approve)');
}
```

and `resolveEffectiveDecision(marker, contract, headUnresolvable)` (third arg, wired from `wireEffectiveDecision`) refuses the approve classification while trusted records sit on an unresolvable head:

```js
if (headUnresolvable && parserClass === 'approve') parserClass = 'rework';
```

Semantics pinned by new tests:

- **AC1h** (the gap you called out, orchestration-level): trusted REQUEST_CHANGES marker + throwing headRefOid transport + a genuine parser `approve` contract → **rework** path (`agent:rework` + recomputed `rework-round-1`), never `pr_approved`, the `::warning::` names the failed probe, and no marker-governs log (no marker matched an unresolvable head).
- **Pure-ladder test**: `resolveEffectiveDecision(null, approve-contract, true)` → `rework` / `legacy-parser`; parser `rework` stays rework; **`false` keeps the AC1d legacy fail-open** (a resolved head still genuinely approves); `unknown` still rides the loud missing-verdict path; `override=true` stays rework either way.

The header ladder doc gained the clause so the invisible list and the fail-safe state stay documented. Round bookeeping rides the existing `decisionNeedsRoundRecompute` path (safe-side rework over an approve contract recomputes `next_round`/`escalate`), so a suppressed approve cannot loop the same round forever.

No sh change needed: the fallback parser (`setup/review-verdict.sh`) has no head probe — the marker read is JS-side — and the sh-parity harness confirms it: **107/107 scenarios byte-identical** after the change. Suite: **115 passed / 0 failed** (113 + AC1h + the pure-ladder case).

